WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:01.470
- [Narrator] This patch of land,

00:00:01.470 --> 00:00:04.260
about 70 miles outside of Houston, Texas

00:00:04.260 --> 00:00:07.110
doesn't look like much but if you zoom in,

00:00:07.110 --> 00:00:10.440
these little rectangles sit
above a powerful economic

00:00:10.440 --> 00:00:12.090
and political tool.

00:00:12.090 --> 00:00:15.600
Just underground is a network
of caverns full of oil,

00:00:15.600 --> 00:00:19.830
part of the US's Strategic
Petroleum Reserve or SPR.

00:00:19.830 --> 00:00:21.570
Since March, President Biden

00:00:21.570 --> 00:00:24.180
has been tapping into it in a big way.

00:00:24.180 --> 00:00:25.680
- The Department of Energy released

00:00:25.680 --> 00:00:27.320
another 15 million barrels

00:00:27.320 --> 00:00:29.580
from the Strategy Petroleum Reserve.

00:00:29.580 --> 00:00:31.230
- [Narrator] All together,
the administration

00:00:31.230 --> 00:00:35.100
will have released 180
million barrels in 2022,

00:00:35.100 --> 00:00:38.070
the largest drawdown from the SPR ever.

00:00:38.070 --> 00:00:40.740
Pumping from the reserve,
the administration says

00:00:40.740 --> 00:00:43.020
has helped lower Americans' energy bills,

00:00:43.020 --> 00:00:44.820
and stabilize markets.

00:00:44.820 --> 00:00:48.120
But some oil industry experts disagree.

00:00:48.120 --> 00:00:49.680
- When we talk about the forces

00:00:49.680 --> 00:00:51.180
that are hitting the oil market,

00:00:51.180 --> 00:00:54.000
this does very little to change the tide.

00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:56.130
- [Narrator] Here's how the
Strategic Petroleum Reserve

00:00:56.130 --> 00:00:58.740
can be used to try to bring down prices,

00:00:58.740 --> 00:01:02.103
and why some are skeptical
that Biden's moves are working.

00:01:02.103 --> 00:01:03.600
(pensive music)

00:01:03.600 --> 00:01:05.700
Along the Texas and Louisiana coasts,

00:01:05.700 --> 00:01:08.280
the caverns are located in Bryan Mound,

00:01:08.280 --> 00:01:11.463
Big Hill, West Hackberry,
and Bayou Choctaw.

00:01:12.420 --> 00:01:13.800
As of October 28th,

00:01:13.800 --> 00:01:18.240
the reserve held about 399
million barrels of oil.

00:01:18.240 --> 00:01:19.073
- We're talking about sort

00:01:19.073 --> 00:01:23.010
of four storage sites, 60 caverns.

00:01:23.010 --> 00:01:25.290
The largest one I think is big enough

00:01:25.290 --> 00:01:27.640
that you could fit the Willis Tower

00:01:27.640 --> 00:01:29.550
that you have in Chicago in it.

00:01:29.550 --> 00:01:33.244
And the tower four for
reference is 1,451 feet.

00:01:33.244 --> 00:01:34.590
That's substantial.

00:01:34.590 --> 00:01:36.420
You could put a lot of oil in there.

00:01:36.420 --> 00:01:38.190
- [Narrator] So why does
the US government keep

00:01:38.190 --> 00:01:40.080
this much oil on hand?

00:01:40.080 --> 00:01:41.760
It goes back to the Energy Policy

00:01:41.760 --> 00:01:44.640
and Conservation Act of 1975.

00:01:44.640 --> 00:01:48.420
During the 1973 to '74 OPEC oil embargo,

00:01:48.420 --> 00:01:51.240
prices for oil skyrocketed.

00:01:51.240 --> 00:01:53.190
- As a result, we face the possibility

00:01:53.190 --> 00:01:54.750
of temporary fuel shortages

00:01:54.750 --> 00:01:57.990
and some increases in
fuel prices in America.

00:01:57.990 --> 00:01:59.910
- [Narrator] This led
to gasoline shortages

00:01:59.910 --> 00:02:00.930
across the country.

00:02:00.930 --> 00:02:01.763
- No gas.

00:02:02.873 --> 00:02:04.380
- There's no gas today?
- No.

00:02:04.380 --> 00:02:06.870
- [Narrator] When President
Ford signed the act into law,

00:02:06.870 --> 00:02:08.640
it was an attempt to establish a buffer

00:02:08.640 --> 00:02:11.310
against future supply shocks of this kind.

00:02:11.310 --> 00:02:13.980
It specified that presidents
can tap into the reserves

00:02:13.980 --> 00:02:16.440
when there's an energy supply interruption

00:02:16.440 --> 00:02:18.870
or due to obligations of the United States

00:02:18.870 --> 00:02:21.420
under the international energy program.

00:02:21.420 --> 00:02:23.910
Over the years, that's meant a few things.

00:02:23.910 --> 00:02:26.640
- Historically, the US
has drawn to the SPR

00:02:26.640 --> 00:02:29.250
for emergency and non-emergency releases.

00:02:29.250 --> 00:02:30.330
What's an emergency release?

00:02:30.330 --> 00:02:32.190
It's a hurricane that
disrupts all production.

00:02:32.190 --> 00:02:33.990
And what's a non-emergency drawdown?

00:02:33.990 --> 00:02:36.990
It's a sale mandated by Congress as part

00:02:36.990 --> 00:02:37.830
of policy making.

00:02:37.830 --> 00:02:38.940
- [Narrator] Using that authority,

00:02:38.940 --> 00:02:41.610
Biden tapped into the reserves
starting in the spring

00:02:41.610 --> 00:02:43.020
and through this fall.

00:02:43.020 --> 00:02:45.090
- We 're gonna continue
to stabilize markets

00:02:45.090 --> 00:02:48.000
and decrease the prices at a time

00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:49.770
when the actions of other countries

00:02:49.770 --> 00:02:51.600
have caused such volatility.

00:02:51.600 --> 00:02:54.570
- The market for oil has been very tight.

00:02:54.570 --> 00:02:59.570
And the US has had to balance
the fight against inflation,

00:02:59.910 --> 00:03:02.700
and gasoline prices has
been a main driver of that

00:03:02.700 --> 00:03:05.130
with targeting Russia and punishing it

00:03:05.130 --> 00:03:07.620
for its invasion of Ukraine.

00:03:07.620 --> 00:03:10.620
- [Narrator] Releases from the
SPR can help calm oil traders

00:03:10.620 --> 00:03:14.760
by injecting more crude
supplies into global markets.

00:03:14.760 --> 00:03:18.840
Right now, oil supplies are
tight, causing high prices.

00:03:18.840 --> 00:03:20.700
By taking oil from the reserves

00:03:20.700 --> 00:03:23.280
and selling it to traders and refiners,

00:03:23.280 --> 00:03:25.050
Biden is increasing supply

00:03:25.050 --> 00:03:27.270
in an effort to lower prices at the pump

00:03:27.270 --> 00:03:28.590
because gasoline prices

00:03:28.590 --> 00:03:31.260
are largely determined by oil prices.

00:03:31.260 --> 00:03:33.300
This strategy has worked in the past.

00:03:33.300 --> 00:03:35.280
Take when the government
tapped into the reserve

00:03:35.280 --> 00:03:38.010
after Hurricane Katrina as an example.

00:03:38.010 --> 00:03:39.960
The US sold 11 million barrels

00:03:39.960 --> 00:03:42.780
to keep production study
for the rest of the country.

00:03:42.780 --> 00:03:45.510
After the release, prices stabilized.

00:03:45.510 --> 00:03:47.940
And something similar
happened earlier this year due

00:03:47.940 --> 00:03:49.470
to Biden's March announcement

00:03:49.470 --> 00:03:52.320
when he said he'd release
one million barrels a day.

00:03:52.320 --> 00:03:54.090
- Treasury Department estimates

00:03:54.090 --> 00:03:57.240
that the releases that
we've seen this year,

00:03:57.240 --> 00:03:58.920
lower the price of gasoline

00:03:58.920 --> 00:04:02.160
between 13 and 31 cents per gallon.

00:04:02.160 --> 00:04:04.080
So that's a pretty broad range.

00:04:04.080 --> 00:04:06.600
- [Narrator] But industry
critics say that the oil releases

00:04:06.600 --> 00:04:09.360
have artificially lowered
prices in the short term,

00:04:09.360 --> 00:04:12.300
dissuading investment
in new oil production.

00:04:12.300 --> 00:04:15.300
US officials said they had
intended the reserve releases

00:04:15.300 --> 00:04:18.330
to be a stop gap until
domestic production ramped up.

00:04:18.330 --> 00:04:20.790
- There's only be a slight
increase in production

00:04:20.790 --> 00:04:24.210
so far this year, about 6% since January.

00:04:24.210 --> 00:04:28.230
And that's largely because
the large producers in the US

00:04:28.230 --> 00:04:30.480
have been conservative about spending.

00:04:30.480 --> 00:04:32.640
- [Narrator] Biden has tried
to deal with lower production

00:04:32.640 --> 00:04:35.370
by announcing that he
would refill the reserve.

00:04:35.370 --> 00:04:37.530
- That means all companies can invest

00:04:37.530 --> 00:04:39.660
to ramp up production now

00:04:39.660 --> 00:04:41.880
with confidence they'll
be able to sell their oil

00:04:41.880 --> 00:04:45.990
to us at that price in the future, $70.

00:04:45.990 --> 00:04:47.100
- [Narrator] But analysts have said

00:04:47.100 --> 00:04:49.110
that shifting from releasing the reserves

00:04:49.110 --> 00:04:52.260
to refilling them could
remove large amounts of oil

00:04:52.260 --> 00:04:53.700
from the global market,

00:04:53.700 --> 00:04:55.980
which would continue to tighten supplies

00:04:55.980 --> 00:04:57.870
and contribute to inflation.

00:04:57.870 --> 00:05:00.600
They say that Biden should
wait to refill the reserves

00:05:00.600 --> 00:05:04.020
until there's a surplus
of oil and lower prices.

00:05:04.020 --> 00:05:05.492
The latest release from the reserve,

00:05:05.492 --> 00:05:07.680
the 15 million barrels,

00:05:07.680 --> 00:05:09.720
are said to be delivered in December.

00:05:09.720 --> 00:05:11.700
- Biden has said that
he would ask his team

00:05:11.700 --> 00:05:12.930
at the Department of Energy

00:05:12.930 --> 00:05:15.398
to, depending on the situation,

00:05:15.398 --> 00:05:18.480
make further releases from the SPR.

00:05:18.480 --> 00:05:21.480
So he has not ruled out
additional releases.

00:05:21.480 --> 00:05:23.220
- [Narrator] Until the SPR is refilled,

00:05:23.220 --> 00:05:26.640
federal inventories are at a 38-year low

00:05:26.640 --> 00:05:30.589
but Biden says there is still
enough in case of emergency.

00:05:30.589 --> 00:05:33.339
(dramatic music)

